<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luddites</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1811">1811</a>–<a href="/years/1816">1816</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Luddism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Luddites were English textile workers who, between 1811 and 1816, wrecked certain kinds of machinery in organised raids, out of concern for their pay, working conditions and the quality of goods. Named after the legendary Ned Ludd, whose name appeared in threatening letters to mill owners and officials, the movement began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottinghamshire">Nottinghamshire</a> and spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> and the North West. Parliament made machine-breaking a capital crime in 1812, and some 12,000 troops were deployed against the Luddites; a mass trial at York in 1813 led to executions and transportation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luddites.jpg" alt="Luddites" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luddite.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
